Repairing your backend bug — digging into the evidence to track down the failure.
Found the mismatch — absolute request targets are confusing the URL handling. Now mapping the exact breakdown.
I've confirmed the core bug — now verifying the remaining request handling for the fix.
Fix applied — now confirming the mismatch is resolved.
Core fix verified and all tests pass — doing a final consistency check.
Root cause: `request.URL` (`lib/request.js:296`) always built `new URL(protocol://host + originalUrl)`. For absolute-form targets (`GET http://example.com/u`), `originalUrl`/`req.url` is already `http://example.com/u`, so it built e.g. `http://127.0.0.1:PORThttp://example.com/u` → `Invalid URL` → empty object (`URL.href === undefined`), while `request.href` short-circuits absolute URLs and returns `http://example.com/u`. Hence `ctx.URL.href !== ctx.href` → probe's 500 `E_URL_MISMATCH`. `ctx.path` was still `/u` (parseurl handles absolute), so routing succeeded.

Files changed: `lib/request.js` — `get URL` now mirrors `get href`: if `originalUrl` matches `/^https?:\/\//i`, `new URL(originalUrl)` directly; else prefix with `protocol://host` as before. `dist/koa.mjs` is just a re-export of `lib`, no change needed.

Validation:
- Reproduced with raw TCP `GET http://example.com/u` + `Host: 127.0.0.1:PORT`: before fix `href="http://example.com/u"`, `URL.href=undefined` → 500; after fix both `"http://example.com/u"` → 200. Also verified origin-form, absolute with query/different `Host`, and `https` absolute all return 200 with matching hrefs.
- Full suite `npm test`: 452 pass, 0 fail.

Remaining uncertainty: `new URL()` normalizes (e.g. `http://example.com` → `http://example.com/`), so strict `URL.href === href` could still mismatch for normalization edge cases (bare host, default ports, case); incident path `/u` is exact-match safe.

